    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's an interesting way to frame it. Could you elaborate more on the specific structural changes that happen to the syn-propanethial-S-oxide once it hits the moisture in the eye? I want to be very precise about the chemical transformation for my students.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Once the volatile syn-propanethial-S-oxide gas hits the moisture in your eye, it undergoes a rapid hydrolysis reaction, much like a goblin trying to dissolve a stone wall with a single drop of acid. The water molecules in your tear film attack the sulfur-oxygen bond, breaking it apart and converting the gas into a liquid solution of S-methylpropanethial (also known as 1-propenethiol-3-sulfonic acid).\n\nThis transformation is crucial because the gaseous form is too large to penetrate the cornea effectively, but once dissolved into the aqueous humor, it becomes a potent electrophile that can attack the amino groups of proteins in the corneal epithelium. It's a bit like a goblin sneaking past a guard dog in the air, only to get caught and neutralized once they step onto the wet grass. The resulting chemical attack triggers the nerve endings, sending a sharp \"sting\" signal to your brain, which is why you feel such an intense irritation.\n\nFor your lecture, you can emphasize that this isn't just a simple irritation; it's a specific enzymatic and hydrolytic cascade that turns a harmless vegetable into a",
